Who unrolls a loop over a constant iterable: a target whose view is markup,
and no other.

html writes the three rows into the page, because nothing on a static page can
run a loop -- and it does so after lowering, one document at a time
(optimize.Documents), since the optimizer unrolls nothing for any target. The
const is then a value the build unrolled against rather than one the page
reads, so shake keeps it on pkg.BuildConsts and the page declares no `rows`.

Every target with a host language emits the loop itself and leaves unrolling
to its own compiler, which can see the bounds are constant: writing the copies
out makes the generated source bigger and no faster. The limit on what html
will unroll is unroll_policy.txt's.

deny out/none/html/index.html `\bfor\b` -- html writes the three rows as markup; no loop reaches the page
deny out/none/html/index.html `\brows\b` -- the const is the build's: nothing on the page reads it
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `Render\(fmt\.Sprint\("` -- a row is rendered from the loop variable, not from a literal the build wrote out
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `SetText\("` -- a row's label is set from the loop variable, not from a literal the build wrote out
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `Text\(text = "` -- a row's Text reads the loop variable, not a literal the build wrote out

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

const rows list<string> = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"]

window {
    vbox {
        for var row = rows {
            text(value=row)
        }
    }
}
